course: dictionary
lesson: 11095
title: One word, two jobs: wheel
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can be a thing and an action. Look at the words around them to find out which. «Wheeled carts appeared about 5,500 years ago» is about things: carts had wheels, so wheel is a round thing that turns.
explain: Now look at «To wheel something is to push it along on wheels.» Here wheel is a doing word. Ask yourself: is the sentence talking about a thing or about an action? The little words, like «to» and «the», give you the clue.
explain: Phrases carry meaning too. «Behind the wheel» means driving a car, even though no wheel is in sight. Read the whole phrase, not just one word, and it makes sense.
fact: A wheel is a round thing that turns on a rod so a car, bike or cart can move.
fact: A steering wheel is the wheel a driver turns.
fact: «Wheeled carts appeared about 5,500 years ago, long after farming and pottery.»
fact: In maths, a bicycle has 2 wheels, a tricycle has 3 and a car has 4.
fact: «Behind the wheel» means driving, and «on wheels» means moving on wheels.
exercise: In «9 cycles and 9 × 2 = 18 wheels», how many wheels does each cycle have? | 3 | 1 | *2 | 4
exercise: «Behind the wheel» means which? | *Driving a car | Reading a map in a shop | Standing still in the rain | Eating a meal
exercise: «To wheel something is to push it along on wheels.» What does to wheel mean? | Lift it high into the air | Paint it bright red | *Push it along on wheels | Put it in a box
test: «A fast train can need over a kilometre to stop, because it is very heavy and steel wheels on steel rails have little grip.» Why does the train need so far to stop? | It has no wheels at all | It is very light and the wheels have lots of grip | It is going backwards | *It is very heavy and the steel wheels have little grip
test: In «The rails steer the wheels, so the driver can only make the train go faster or slower», what does the sentence tell you about a train? | A train can turn round any corner it likes | A train can fly over obstacles | A train has a steering wheel like a car | *A train can never swerve round anything on the line
exam: In «The water wheel turned slowly in the river», which word is a thing and which is an action? | Both words are things | *Wheel is a thing here and turned is the action | Wheel is the action and turned is the thing | Both words are actions
